  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 250 AM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nThrough tonight: Dry weather and a gradual warming trend is \nexpected. Early morning satellite imagery/RAP analysis showed the \ncirculation around a low spinning over the northern Gulf. This low \nwas resulting in some mid to high level clouds across the eastern \nhalf of our CWA but no rainfall. This low is expected to shift east \nthrough tonight. Early morning surface analysis had a 1028mb high \ncentered over the eastern seaboard that the will continue to ridge \nback across the Gulf coast states through tonight. This will \nmaintain a northeast to east flow across our region essentially \nkeeping the Gulf closed. With a little more insolation today \ntemperatures will top out a degree or two warmer than on Thursday. \nMorning lows Saturday will be a degree or two warmer than this \nmorning. /22/\n\nSaturday - Wednesday\n\nDry conditions will continue into Saturday as a sfc high migrate \neastward. As the high moves out, the next chances for rain will \nprovide by an incoming cold front on Sunday. As a cold front pushes \ninto the area Sunday into Monday, rain chances range between 15-60% \nwith the greatest chances in the MS Delta and along/north of HWY 82.\nAlthough any received rain will be helpful; rainfall total won't be \nenough to relive ongoing drought conditions. \n\nPost-frontal passage will cool temperatures to below seasonal \naverages (5-10 degrees cooler) heading into the Thanksgiving holiday.\nA brief shortwave disturbance may bring an isolated (25% or less) \nshower or two to NE MS on Wednesday but the Thanksgiving holiday is \nexpected to be dry and quiet. \n\nOutside of rain chances for the ArkLaMiss region Sunday into Monday, \nNHC is monitoring for tropical cyclone development (around 50%) over \nthe weekend and next week as an upper-lvl low moves along the \nAtlantic coastline. No impacts are expected for our region. /SW/",
